Objects are created with curly braces and read with dot notation for known keys or bracket notation for dynamic keys.

code:
const user = { name: 'Ana', age: 30 };
console.log(user.name);
const key = 'age';
console.log(user[key]);  // 30

real example:
Reading fields off an API response like res.body.checkout.total is nothing more than chained dot notation.

Assign to the property with dot or bracket notation. Assigning to a new key adds it, and to an existing key overwrites it.

code:
const user = {};
user.name = 'Ana';
user['age'] = 30;
console.log(user);  // { name: 'Ana', age: 30 }

real example:
Merging runtime data into a base request body by assignment keeps API fixtures flexible per test.

Spreading an object copies its enumerable properties into a new object, so spread order decides which keys win when merging.

code:
const base = { browser: 'chromium', headless: true };
const override = { headless: false };
const config = { ...base, ...override };
console.log(config);  // { browser: 'chromium', headless: false }

real example:
Merging default launch options with per-test overrides keeps shared config clean.

A shallow copy duplicates the top level but shares nested objects by reference, while a deep copy replicates all nested levels.

code:
const a = { x: { y: 1 } };
const shallow = { ...a };
shallow.x.y = 2;
console.log(a.x.y);  // 2

real example:
Copying test config with nested objects silently shares them, causing cross-test mutation.

structuredClone is the modern deep copy, while JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) works for JSON-safe values.

code:
const copy = structuredClone(original);
const jsonCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));

real example:
Cloning payload fixtures before mutating keeps the original test data intact across runs.

A method defined in an object gets this bound to the object when called as obj.method(), but the binding is lost when the method is passed as a bare reference.

code:
const counter = {
  n: 0,
  inc() { this.n++; }
};
counter.inc();
console.log(counter.n);  // 1

real example:
Playwright page objects rely on this staying bound inside methods, so arrow wrappers are used in callbacks.
